This section describes how to download a file by using the Portal UI. The functionality must be executed through an action and that action return in the ReturnMsg variable a tag '#FileDownload#' together with a JSON string that contains information to the UI to build a proper dialog to the user and download the file. One of the types of information to set in the JSON configuration is a name of a stored procedure the UI is calling when the download is done. The download can succeed or fail and the call to that stored procedure secure to be in charge of what should happen.

The JSON string has the following members.

| Parameter |Description |
|--|--|
| FileNameIn | The path and filename of the file to download. The user running the IIS Application Pool must have access to that file on the filesystem |
| FileNameOut |The name of the dowloaded file places in the default download folder.  |
| DownloadID | This is a reference ID (GUID) that is returned on the 'OnCompleteProc' when the download is done. That can be used to store in a table or on that record the user executes a download and then when the download returns the 'DownloadID' it can then be used to lookup the record again used for changing som status and/or creating a Changelog with information about the status of the download. |
| OnCompleteProc | This is the name of the stored procedure to call when the download succeed or fail.   |
| MessageBoxText | The body text of the dialog the user gets to accept a download. |
| HeaderText | The header text in the dialog. |
| DownloadText | The message in the upper right corner of the UI when the file downloads |
| DownloadErrorText | The text the user will get when the download fail. This is not the specific error message but something about an error occurred and to check the changelog |
| ButtonText | The caption on the download button |

In this example there is created a configtable called 'FileDownload' with columns 'ID', 'Description', 'FileName' and 'NoOfDownloads'. This is just as a demo. The user can create a record and download the given file and when succeeded the 'NoOfDownloads' is increased by 1. The download is then logged a complete.

The table is defined like this:
```
CREATE TABLE [config].[FileDownload](
                             [ID] [uniqueidentifier] NOT NULL,
                             [Description] [nvarchar](50) NOT NULL,
                             [FileName] [nvarchar](500) NOT NULL,
                             [NoOfDownloads] [int] NOT NULL,
                             [DW_ArchiveID] [bigint] NULL,
                             [DW_Audit_Insert] [int] NULL,
                             [DW_Audit_Update] [int] NULL,
                             [DW_Audit] [int] NULL,
 CONSTRAINT [pk_config_FileDownload] PRIMARY KEY NONCLUSTERED
(
                             [ID] ASC
)WITH (PAD_INDEX = OFF, STATISTICS_NORECOMPUTE = OFF, IGNORE_DUP_KEY = OFF, ALLOW_ROW_LOCKS = ON, ALLOW_PAGE_LOCKS = ON, OPTIMIZE_FOR_SEQUENTIAL_KEY = OFF) ON [PRIMARY]
) ON [PRIMARY]
GO

ALTER TABLE [config].[FileDownload] ADD  CONSTRAINT [DF_FileDownload_DW_ArchiveID]  DEFAULT (NEXT VALUE FOR [config].[ID_FileDownload]) FOR [DW_ArchiveID]
GO
```

The action that downloads the file:

```
ALTER PROCEDURE [config].[FileDownload_Action_DownloadFile]
     (@Columns nvarchar(max),
      @Values nvarchar(max),                               
      @ReturnMsg nvarchar(max) OUTPUT)
AS

DECLARE @DW_Audit as int = -1;

DECLARE @Language nvarchar(20) = (SELECT [config].[PortalGetLanguage]());
DECLARE @Username nvarchar(50) = (SELECT [config].[PortalGetUsername]());

declare @ConfigTable as nvarchar(250) = (select Item from dbo.SplitString((SELECT OBJECT_NAME(@@PROCID)),'_') where Num = 1);

SET @ReturnMsg = '';

BEGIN TRY   
    DECLARE @DW_ArchiveID as bigint;

    declare @DWArchiveList as nvarchar(max) = (select config.PortalGetValue(@Columns,@Values,'DW_ArchiveID'));

    declare @JSON as nvarchar(max) = '';   
    declare @DownloadID as uniqueidentifier;
    declare @FileNameIn as nvarchar(max);
    declare @FileNameOut as nvarchar(max);
    declare @OnCompleteProc as nvarchar(max);
    declare @MessageBoxText as nvarchar(max);                
    declare @HeaderText as nvarchar(max);
    declare @DownloadText as nvarchar(max);
    declare @DownloadErrorText as nvarchar(max);
    declare @ButtonText as nvarchar(max);

    set @DownloadID = cast((select config.PortalGetValue(@Columns,@Values,'ID')) as uniqueidentifier);
    set @FileNameIn = (select config.PortalGetValue(@Columns,@Values,'FileName'));
    set @FileNameOut = (select item from dbo.SplitString(@FileNameIn,'\') where num = 3);
    set @OnCompleteProc = 'config.FileDownload_OnDownloadFileCompleted';
    set @MessageBoxText = 
          CHAR(13) + CHAR(10)
                                 + 'Press the button to dowload the file : ' + CHAR(13) + CHAR(10) + @FileNameIn + CHAR(13) + CHAR(10) +' into the download f folder..';

    set @HeaderText = 'Download file';
    set @DownloadText = 'The file "'  + @FileNameOut + '" is being downloaded...';
    set @DownloadErrorText = 'The download fails. Check the changelog for further information';
    set @ButtonText = 'Download';

    drop table if exists #json
    select
                               @HeaderText as HeaderText,
                               @MessageBoxText as MessageBoxText,
                               @ButtonText as ButtonText,
                               @DownloadText as DownloadText,
                               @DownloadErrorText as DownloadErrorText,
                               @DownloadID as ID,
                               @FileNameIn as FileNameIn,
                               @FileNameOut as FileNameOut,
                               @OnCompleteProc as OnCompleteProc
                             into
                               #json

   set @JSON = (select * from #json for json auto, WITHOUT_ARRAY_WRAPPER);

   set @ReturnMsg = '#FileDownload#' + @JSON;
   RETURN 0;

END TRY
BEGIN CATCH
  SET @ReturnMsg = ERROR_MESSAGE();
  RETURN 1;
END CATCH
```

When the file is downloaded with or without an error the Portal UI will execute a stored procedure to tell that the operation is completed. That can be useful in situation where a status column must be updated with 'Success' or 'Error' or just in case an action changelog must be created.

In this example the 'NoOfDownloads' is increased by 1 that will create a modify changelog and a further log is created telling if the download has succeeded or failed.

```
CREATE PROCEDURE [config].[FileDownload_OnDownloadFileCompleted]
      (
      @ID uniqueidentifier, 
      @ErrorMsg as nvarchar(max)
      ) 
AS

DECLARE @Language nvarchar(20) = (SELECT [config].[PortalGetLanguage]());
DECLARE @Username nvarchar(50) = (SELECT [config].[PortalGetUsername]());
DECLARE @ConfigTable as nvarchar(250) = (select Item from dbo.SplitString((SELECT OBJECT_NAME(@@PROCID)),'_') where Num = 1);

BEGIN TRY
  declare @DW_ArchiveID as bigint = isnull((select DW_ArchiveID from config.FileDownload where ID = @ID),-1);

  if @DW_ArchiveID > 0
  begin
    declare @ReturnMsg as nvarchar(max);

    declare @Ok as bit = 1;
    if isnull(@ErrorMsg,'') <> ''
      set @Ok = 0;

    if @Ok = 1
    begin
      declare @NoOfDownload as int = isnull((select NoOfDownloads from config.FileDownload where DW_ArchiveID = @DW_ArchiveID),0);

      set @NoOfDownload += 1;

      EXECUTE [config].[PortalModifyOneColumn]
                                                             @ConfigTable = @ConfigTable
                                                            ,@RecordID = @DW_ArchiveID
                                                            ,@Columnname = 'NoOfDownloads'
                                                            ,@Value = @NoOfDownload
                                                            ,@ReturnMsg = @ReturnMsg OUTPUT

    end;

    declare @DW_Audit as bigint
    DECLARE @Project as nvarchar(50);
    DECLARE @TaskName as nvarchar(50);

    SET @Project = (select config.PortalGetProjectname());

    SET @TaskName = 'FileDownload_Action_DownloadFile_' + cast(@DW_ArchiveiD as nvarchar(10));

    EXEC [config].[PortalChangeLog_Start]
                @Project = @Project,       
                @TaskName = @TaskName,
                @Username = @Username,
                @Columns = N'',
                @NewValues = N'',
                @OldValues = N'',
                @Audit_ID = @DW_Audit OUTPUT

    if @Ok = 1
      EXEC [config].[PortalChangeLog_Ok] @Audit_ID = @DW_Audit,@Changes = 1, @Message = N'';
    else
      EXEC [config].[PortalChangeLog_Error] @RecordID = @DW_ArchiveID, @Audit_ID = @DW_Audit,@Message = @ErrorMsg;   
  end;

END TRY
BEGIN CATCH
  THROW;
  RETURN 1;
END CATCH

RETURN 0;

```



'Actionform' is an advanced feature in the AdminPortal that makes it possible to create a new form that will open after pushing an action button. This form can be a physical record in a specific table, or it can be more like a temporary wizard table or something in between. It depends on the situation, but it will often be used to automate functionality to make the use of the application more effective.

After pushing the 'Save' button on the form some business logic can run on one or more records in the base table depending on if this specific action is a multi-action or a single action.

The first step is to create a normal action. See the # [Module Dev Guide Config](https://kmddk.sharepoint.com/sites/Dima-365/Delte%20dokumenter/KMD.DIMA.KIP/Datawarehousing/Guidelines%20and%20How-Tos/03%20Module%20Dev%20Guide%20Config.docx?web=1) how to do that. The action can be placed as a single-action that means on each record (Placement = 0 or 1) or it can be a multi-action running in selected records (Placement 2 or 3)

_Step 1 : Create an Action button_

```
CREATE PROCEDURE [config].[#ConfigTableName#_Action_NameOfAction]
     (-- Standard system variables
                               @Columns nvarchar(max),
                               @Values nvarchar(max),

                              -- ReturnMsg
                               @ReturnMsg nvarchar(max) OUTPUT)
AS

DECLARE @Language nvarchar(20) = (SELECT [config].[PortalGetLanguage]());
DECLARE @Username nvarchar(50) = (SELECT [config].[PortalGetUsername]());

declare @ConfigTable as nvarchar(250) = (select Item from dbo.SplitString((SELECT OBJECT_NAME(@@PROCID)),'_') where Num = 1);

SET @ReturnMsg = '';

BEGIN TRY

                             -- If this fail with -1 it is called from a multi-selected function
                             DECLARE @DW_ArchiveID as bigint = ISNULL(TRY_CONVERT(bigint,(select config.PortalGetValue(@Columns,@Values,'DW_ArchiveID'))),-1);

                             DECLARE @NoOfSelectedRows as bigint = ISNULL(TRY_CONVERT(bigint,(select config.PortalGetValue(@Columns,@Values,'NoOfSelectedRows'))),-1);
                             DECLARE @NoOfRows as bigint = ISNULL(TRY_CONVERT(bigint,(select config.PortalGetValue(@Columns,@Values,'NoOfRows'))),-1);

                             --Check if @NoOfSelectedRows make sense in this scenario
                             --If Not return a message in @ReturnMsg and return -1
                             -- Otherwise start the Actionform by returning like below.

                             set @ReturnMsg = '#ActionForm#';

                             RETURN 0;
END TRY
BEGIN CATCH
  SET @ReturnMsg = ERROR_MESSAGE();
  RETURN 1;
END CATCH

RETURN 0;

```
The only thing this action does is to return in @ReturnMsg that the UI now should handle this as an 'ActionForm'. Of course, it would be possible to have more business logic inside this action for example preparation for the next step, but normally that will not be necessary and therefore will code like this also do it:

```
CREATE PROCEDURE [config].[#ConfigTableName#_Action_NameOfAction]
     (-- Standard system variables
                               @Columns nvarchar(max),
                               @Values nvarchar(max),

                              -- ReturnMsg
                               @ReturnMsg nvarchar(max) OUTPUT)
AS

set @ReturnMsg = '#ActionForm#';
RETURN 0;

```

_Step 2 : Create an OnLoadActionForm_

The UI will now call another stored procedure called '[config].[#ConfigTableName#_OnLoadActionForm]'. A template for that can be found here 'include\reuse_config\sql\00_CFG\Template\Optional\config.ConfigTable_OnLoadActionForm.StoredProcedure.sql'

This stored procedure will get information about which records this action is running on and that can be a single-record or multi-records. The job for this stored procedure is to return information to the UI, so the UI can build a single-record card form and present that to the end-user. That could be almost anything - it could be a record in an existing table, or it could be a temporary table record just defined for this specific action - like a wizard kind of thing.

It is important to remember that from the opening of that card-form, it is implemented just like all other card-form in **Edit-mode (Modify)** - so that will not be covered further in this section. Despite it is a normal card-form it will not have a 'Delete' button nor a 'Changelog' button.

_Example 1 : Just to start with_
This example will properly not be used i the real world but I will let the user work on an existing table on a specific record to change the data and save the record.

```
CREATE OR ALTER PROCEDURE [config].[#ConfigTableName#_OnLoadActionForm]
      (-- Standard system variables

                               @ActionName nvarchar(250),                       
                               @Columns nvarchar(max),
                               @Values nvarchar(max),
                               @ActionFormHeaderText nvarchar(max) OUTPUT,
                               @ActionFormConfigTable nvarchar(250) OUTPUT,
                               @ActionFormColumns nvarchar(max) OUTPUT,
                               @ActionFormValues nvarchar(max) OUTPUT,
                               @ReturnMsg nvarchar(max) OUTPUT
                               ) 
AS

DECLARE @Language nvarchar(20) = (SELECT [config].[PortalGetLanguage]());
DECLARE @Username nvarchar(250) = (SELECT [config].[PortalGetUsername]());
DECLARE @ConfigTable as nvarchar(250) = (select Item from dbo.SplitString((SELECT OBJECT_NAME(@@PROCID)),'_') where Num = 1);

set @Values = replace(@Values,'''','''''');

set @ReturnMsg = '';

BEGIN TRY

  set @ActionFormConfigTable = '';
  set @ActionFormHeaderText = '';
  set @ActionFormColumns = ''
  set @ActionFormValues = '';

  if @ActionName = '<MyExampleAction>'
  begin
    set @ActionFormConfigTable = 'ExistingTable';
    set @ActionFormHeaderText = @ActionName + '_Header';
    set @ActionFormColumns = ''
    set @ActionFormValues = '';

    EXECUTE  [config].[PortalRecordToColumnValue]
                                @ConfigTable = @ActionFormConfigTable
                               ,@RecordID = 513
                               ,@Columns = @ActionFormColumns OUTPUT
                               ,@Values = @ActionFormValues OUTPUT
                               ,@ReturnMsg = @ReturnMsg OUTPUT

  end;

  return 0;
END TRY
BEGIN CATCH
  SET @ReturnMsg = ERROR_MESSAGE();
  RETURN 1;
END CATCH

RETURN 0;

```
This example checks on the @ActionName because if we have more than one action that can open an 'ActionForm' we need to check on the name.

In this example the OnModify i handled already because this is an already existing table.

_Example 2
This examlpe will properly be the most common use of an ActionForm and it also requires a little more use of code. Let’s says we have a scenario where we want to do something on several records - but before we can do that, the user has to enter more information that can be used during this iteration of selected rows. Look at it like a wizard to help the process.

You still must create a 'OnLoadActionForm' like in the first example, but it looks a little bit different because we must create a table definition for that temporary table we use. It could like the code example below.

In this example we create a temporary table called 'ExampleWizard' with 4 columns named 'Column1', 'Column2', 'Column3' and 'Column4'. They are all set to required and one column is as date column, and one is a lookup column. Some of the columns are set with default values.

```
ALTER OR CREATE PROCEDURE [config].[#ConfigTableName#_OnLoadActionForm]
      (-- Standard system variables

                               @ActionName nvarchar(250),                       
                               @Columns nvarchar(max),
                               @Values nvarchar(max),
                               @ActionFormHeaderText nvarchar(max) OUTPUT,
                               @ActionFormConfigTable nvarchar(250) OUTPUT,
                               @ActionFormColumns nvarchar(max) OUTPUT,
                               @ActionFormValues nvarchar(max) OUTPUT,
                               @ReturnMsg nvarchar(max) OUTPUT
                               ) 
AS

DECLARE @Language nvarchar(20) = (SELECT [config].[PortalGetLanguage]());
DECLARE @Username nvarchar(250) = (SELECT [config].[PortalGetUsername]());
DECLARE @ConfigTable as nvarchar(250) = (select Item from dbo.SplitString((SELECT OBJECT_NAME(@@PROCID)),'_') where Num = 1);

set @Values = replace(@Values,'''','''''');

set @ReturnMsg = '';

BEGIN TRY

  set @ActionFormConfigTable = '';
  set @ActionFormHeaderText = '';
  set @ActionFormColumns = ''
  set @ActionFormValues = '';

  if @ActionName = 'MyActionName'
  begin
    set @ActionFormConfigTable = 'ExampleWizard'; -- this is a not existing table
    set @ActionFormHeaderText = @ActionName + '_Wizard';

    -- drop/create fieldlist
                             delete from config.PortalFieldList where ConfigTable = @ActionFormConfigTable;

                             -- Insert the fields in the metadata
                             INSERT INTO [config].[PortalFieldList]
           ([ConfigTable]
           ,[ColumnId]
           ,[SortOrder]     
           ,[ColumnName]
           ,[DataType]
           ,[DataSize]
           ,[FriendlyName]
           ,[IsModifyField]
                                ,[IsRequired]
           ,[IsLookupField]
           ,[LookupInputType]
           ,[LookupRelatedColumn]
           ,[NULL2Value]          
           ,[IsProtectedOnModify]                                                      
                                                            )
                              SELECT
            @ActionFormConfigTable as [ConfigTable]
           ,1001 as [ColumnId]
           ,1001 as [SortOrder]          
           ,'Column1' as [ColumnName]
           ,'nvarchar' as [DataType]
           ,50 as [DataSize]
           ,'Column1' as [FriendlyName]
           ,1 AS [IsModifyField]
                                ,1 as [IsRequired]
           ,0 AS [IsLookupField]
           ,0 AS [LookupInputType]
           ,'' AS [LookupRelatedColumn]
           ,'' AS [NULL2Value]
           ,1 AS [IsProtectedOnModify]                                               
                              UNION
                              SELECT
            @ActionFormConfigTable as [ConfigTable]
           ,1002 as [ColumnId]
           ,1002 as [SortOrder]
           ,'Column2' as [ColumnName]
           ,'nvarchar' as [DataType]
           ,50 as [DataSize]
           ,'Column2' as [FriendlyName]
           ,1 AS [IsModifyField]
                                ,1 as [IsRequired]
           ,0 AS [IsLookupField]
           ,0 AS [LookupInputType]
           ,'' AS [LookupRelatedColumn]
           ,'' AS [NULL2Value]
           ,0 AS [IsProtectedOnModify]                                               
                              UNION
                              SELECT
            @ActionFormConfigTable as [ConfigTable]
           ,1003 as [ColumnId]
           ,1003 as [SortOrder]
           ,'Column3' as [ColumnName]
           ,'nvarchar' as [DataType]
           ,50 as [DataSize]
           ,'Column3' as [FriendlyName]
           ,1 AS [IsModifyField]
                                ,1 as [IsRequired]
           ,1 AS [IsLookupField]
           ,3 AS [LookupInputType]
           ,'' AS [LookupRelatedColumn]
           ,'' AS [NULL2Value]
           ,0 AS [IsProtectedOnModify]                                               
                              UNION
                              SELECT
            @ActionFormConfigTable as [ConfigTable]
           ,1004 as [ColumnId]
           ,1004 as [SortOrder]
           ,'Column4' as [ColumnName]
           ,'date' as [DataType]
           ,50 as [DataSize]
           ,'Column4' as [FriendlyName]
           ,1 AS [IsModifyField]
                                ,1 as [IsRequired]
           ,0 AS [IsLookupField]
           ,0 AS [LookupInputType]
           ,'' AS [LookupRelatedColumn]
           ,'' AS [NULL2Value]
           ,0 AS [IsProtectedOnModify]
         UNION
                              SELECT
            @ActionFormConfigTable as [ConfigTable]
           ,9000 as [ColumnId]
           ,9000 as [SortOrder]
           ,'DW_ArchiveID' as [ColumnName]
           ,'int' as [DataType]
                                ,2 as [DataSize]
           ,'DW_ArchiveID' as [FriendlyName]
           ,0 AS [IsModifyField]
                                ,0 as [IsRequired]
           ,0 AS [IsLookupField]
           ,0 AS [LookupInputType]
           ,'' AS [LookupRelatedColumn]
           ,'' AS [NULL2Value]
           ,0 AS [IsProtectedOnModify]                                               
                              UNION
                              SELECT
            @ActionFormConfigTable as [ConfigTable]
           ,9001 as [ColumnId]
           ,9001 as [SortOrder]
           ,'SelectedRows' as [ColumnName]
           ,'nvarchar' as [DataType]
                                ,0 as [DataSize]
           ,'SelectedRows' as [FriendlyName]
           ,0 AS [IsModifyField]
                                ,1 as [IsRequired]
           ,0 AS [IsLookupField]
           ,0 AS [LookupInputType]
           ,'' AS [LookupRelatedColumn]
           ,'' AS [NULL2Value]
           ,0 AS [IsProtectedOnModify]

     set @ActionFormColumns = 'Column1#¤#Column2#¤#Column3#¤#Columns4#¤#DW_ArchiveID'
     set @ActionFormValues = 'A#¤#B#¤#C#¤##¤#1';

     set @ActionFormColumns = @ActionFormColumns + '#¤#SelectedRows'
     set @ActionFormValues = @ActionFormValues + '#¤#' + isnull((select config.PortalGetValue(@Columns,@Values,'DW_ArchiveID')),''); -- send list of selected records that this action should run through
  end;

  return 0;
END TRY
BEGIN CATCH
  SET @ReturnMsg = ERROR_MESSAGE();
  RETURN 1;
END CATCH

RETURN 0;

```

To handle the lookup column, it is needed to create a stored procedure with lookup values just as with any other lookup column in a config table. In this example the stored procedure is called 'config.ExamlpeWizard_GetValuelist_Column3'

Also notice that this example adds a new column called 'SelectedRows' at the end with information about the records to run through so we with that information and the extra columns can finish the task properly. Since the Actionform is handled like a normal card form in Edit mode the 'Save' button (that can be translated to something more specific) will call the standard stored procedure 'config.PortalGeneral_OnModify' but since this is a not existing table that can only work by overwriting this action with a specific 'OnModify' stored procedure like in this code example:

```
CREATE OR ALTER PROCEDURE [config].[ExamlpeWizard_OnModify]
      (-- Standard system variables

                               @Columns nvarchar(max),
                               @NewValues nvarchar(max),                        
                               @OldValues nvarchar(max) = '',                     

                               -- ReturnMsg
                               @ReturnMsg nvarchar(max) OUTPUT) 

AS
DECLARE @Language nvarchar(20) = (SELECT [config].[PortalGetLanguage]());
DECLARE @Username nvarchar(250) = (SELECT [config].[PortalGetUsername]());

declare @ConfigTable as nvarchar(250) = (select Item from dbo.SplitString((SELECT OBJECT_NAME(@@PROCID)),'_') where Num = 1);

SET @ReturnMsg = '';

BEGIN TRY   

                             DECLARE @SelectedRows as nvarchar(max) = (select config.PortalGetValue(@Columns,@NewValues,'SelectedRows'));

        DECLARE @RowCnt as int = 0;
                             DECLARE @RecordID as bigint;

                             -- Loop through the selected rows and to something
                             WHILE @RowCnt < (select count(*) from dbo.SplitString(@SelectedRows,','))
                             BEGIN
                               SET @RowCnt += 1;
                               SET @RecordID = (select item from dbo.SplitString(@SelectedRows,',') WHERE Num = @RowCnt);

                               -- Now do something with that RecordID and the information from the ActionForm from variables @Columns and @Values

                             END;

END TRY
BEGIN CATCH
  SET @ReturnMsg = ERROR_MESSAGE(); 
  RETURN 1;
END CATCH
```



Logging is a complicated issue and has been changed and improved during the years to make it easier to use and to improve performance. The good thing is that most logging and displaying the log is managed by the portal reuse code.

By default, all tables in the portal have enabled changelog and the action 'Changelog' that can show log history on a table like this:
![image.png](/.attachments/image-7bcbeb5a-9496-458e-aff6-34a81a60f5fb.png)

In this example there are different log types. First an insert log. After that two modify logs and finally an action log. Furthermore, it will also create a delete log but then the only way to find that log after the record is deleted is in the global log view:

![image.png](/.attachments/image-7061a478-5a28-4f62-b72c-95c793c9e2af.png)

The global log can be found here.

![image.png](/.attachments/image-96713cbc-2391-41e8-b340-5e8079ffa33a.png)

 and if it does not exist in the menu, it can be added by adding the table 'PortalModuleLogOverview'

**Log tables**

The portal log is physical placed in two table 'management.PortalLog' and 'management.PortalDetailLog' that is joined together in the changelog window. The 'PortalDetailLog' contains a record per column change that is aggregated into the 'FieldChange' column.

**Manual logging**

Sometimes it is necessary to create a log entry in the code besides what is logged by default. Though if data is manipulated through the standard functions like 'PortalGeneral_OnInsert', 'PortalGeneral_OnModify' and 'PortalGeneral_OnDelete' is used then logging is taking care of but in other situations like working with actions it can be a scenario where writing logging code is necessary.

It can be a good idea to get inspired from the template that is used for actions in the template folder.

`include\reuse_config\sql\00_CFG\Template\Optional\config.ConfigTable_Action_NameOfAction.StoredProcedure.sql`

To create a manual log, follow this step.

_Define the TaskName_

The parameter Taskname is important to get the changelog stored correctly and it must use a defined pattern which is starting with the name of the Configtable and ending with the recordid (DW_ArchvieID). In between the log type that can be 'Action', 'OnInsert', 'OnModify' or 'OnDelete'. If it is an action an actionname must also be part of the Taskname.

Here are some examples:

Action:

`SET @TaskName = 'ConfigTable_Action_NameOfAction_' + cast(@DW_ArchiveiD as nvarchar(10));`

Insert:

`SET @TaskName = 'ConfigTable_OnInsert_' + cast(@DW_ArchiveiD as nvarchar(10));`

Modify:

`SET @TaskName = 'ConfigTable_OnModify_' + cast(@DW_ArchiveiD as nvarchar(10));`

Delete:

`SET @TaskName = 'ConfigTable_OnDelete_' + cast(@DW_ArchiveiD as nvarchar(10));`

_LogStart_

After defining the Taskname the next thing to do is to start a log by calling the stored procedure 'config.PortalChangeLog_Start'. If it i as action it is not possible to send values to 'Columns', 'OldValues' and 'NewValues' since the log type of an Action cannot create field change logs. That will only work with Insert,Modify,Delete logs.

An example of a start logs with an action. This will create the log entry and create a relation to the specific record on that specific configtable. The stored procedure returns a variable @Audit_ID that is used for closing the log with an ok log entry or an error log entry.

```
EXEC [config].[PortalChangeLog_Start]
                @Project = @Project,       
                @TaskName = @TaskName,
                @Username = @Username,
                @Columns = N'',
                @NewValues = N'',
                @OldValues = N'',
                @Audit_ID = @DW_Audit OUTPUT
```

Here are three examples of a log related to Insert, Modify, Delete

Insert:

```
EXEC [config].[PortalChangeLog_Start]
                @Project = @Project,       
                @TaskName = @TaskName,
                @Username = @Username,
                @Columns = @Columns,
                @NewValues = @Values,
                @OldValues = N'',
                @Audit_ID = @DW_Audit OUTPUT
```

Modify:

```
EXEC [config].[PortalChangeLog_Start]
                @Project = @Project,       
                @TaskName = @TaskName,
                @Username = @Username,
                @Columns = @Columns,
                @NewValues = @NewValues,
                @OldValues = @OldValues,
                @Audit_ID = @DW_Audit OUTPUT
```

Delete:

```
EXEC [config].[PortalChangeLog_Start]
                @Project = @Project,       
                @TaskName = @TaskName,
                @Username = @Username,
                @Columns = @Columns,
                @NewValues = N'',
                @OldValues = @OldValues,
                @Audit_ID = @DW_Audit OUTPUT
```

After starting the log some other business logic code is running and when that finish the log must be closed with either an error or an ok log entry.

_Log Ok_

The Ok log entry has a syntax like this. The @Audit_ID must be the variable returned in the previous step. The @Changes is just information and can be number of records changed and the @Message is an information to the user. Typically, is it not necessary to send a message when the operation succeeded but it is possible. Though it is only possible to use @Message in an action log. In a 'Insert', 'Modify', 'Delete' only column changes are logged and the @Message is then ignored.

```
EXEC [config].[PortalChangeLog_Ok] @Audit_ID = @DW_Audit,@Changes = 1, @Message = N'';

```

_Log error_

If an error occurred an error log must be generated, and it looks like this:

```
EXEC [config].[PortalChangeLog_Error] @RecordID = @DW_ArchiveID, @Audit_ID = @DW_Audit,@Message = @ErrorMsg;

```

**Joblog**


Since the changelog in a portal module is now a local log with local tables the information about logging in other modules is not by default enabled in a portal module. Even though the config reuse includes a feature to setup SQL Jobs and monitoring these jobs and dick into flow logs it must be enabled through two synonyms that by default are pointing to an empty view inside the module to avoid reference problems.

The two synonyms we are working with here are _management.JobLog_ and _management.JobLogDetail_. They must be copied from the reuse folder and into the module locale synonyms folder and changed to point on an external log database.

# Introduction

This module installs the <tenant>_00_LOG database onto an ETL node in a solution. This module is a basic part of a solution and all DSA and DataArea and Common modules integrates to the log database by logging activity logs, and by using the Id of the log table as audit id in tables.
In addition to being installed per tenant, it should always be installed for the GLOBAL customer, which allow global modules to log to this one log database per server.

